WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.620 --> 00:00:05.760
NASA, is not only the world's&nbsp;
premier space exploration agency,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:09.720
it's also one of the most recognisable&nbsp;
brands in history. It plays a prominent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:13.800
role in countless films and TV shows, and&nbsp;
its various iconic logos and insignias can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:16.500
be seen on everything from&nbsp;
hot water bottles to hoodies.

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:23.280
Just saying the acronym - NASA - conjures&nbsp;
images of chiselled-jawed astronauts posing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:28.800
beneath shining rocket ships; assorted super&nbsp;
geniuses huddling around computer screens;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:34.260
and a random woman giving a&nbsp;
dolphin a handjob... wait, what?

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:42.480
For all NASA's undeniable excellence, over&nbsp;
the years this storied organisation has had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:48.660
the odd mishap. Like the time the 300&nbsp;
million dollar Mars Climate Orbiter was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:54.300
lost because one of its software systems&nbsp;
used metric units and another imperial.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.200 --> 00:01:00.900
Embarrasingly, nobody even noticed until it&nbsp;
hit Mars' atmosphere, and immediately exploded.

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:08.820
But there’s been no blunder more bonkers&nbsp;
than the batshit-crazy dolphin project that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:15.420
NASA funded in the 1960s. In fact, forget&nbsp;
NASA - this might just be the most absurd&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:20.280
experiment in the history of modern&nbsp;
science, involving interspecies sex,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:25.560
drugs, and a woman living in a flat&nbsp;
share with a dolphin called Peter.

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:31.020
So, buckle up your seat belt and strap&nbsp;
on your snorkel for the titillating tale&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:36.240
of the NASA experiment that went so&nbsp;
wrong it ended up in a porn magazine.

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:12.480
Considering just how bad things got - and we're&nbsp;
talking 'injecting dolphins with LSD' bad - the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:16.680
project had its origins in some genuinely&nbsp;
groundbreaking science - being the brainchild&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:22.080
of a rising star in the fields of psychoanalysis&nbsp;
and neuroscience by the name of Dr. John Lilly.

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:26.280
Lilly was one of the first scientists&nbsp;
to put serious effort into understanding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:30.360
animal intelligence. His early work&nbsp;
largely concerned cats and dogs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:35.400
but it soon became clear that if he wanted to&nbsp;
find true intelligence in the animal kingdom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:39.660
he was going to have to study creatures&nbsp;
with brains as large and complex as our own.

00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:45.120
The bottlenose dolphin proved the perfect&nbsp;
choice. Not only do they do well in captivity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:48.480
their brains are actually bigger&nbsp;
than a human's by about 30%,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:53.880
and their brain to bodyweight ratio,&nbsp;
while smaller than ours, is larger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.880 --> 00:03:59.760
than a chimpanzee's and similar to that of our&nbsp;
human ancestors from around 2 million years ago.

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:03.120
That's an eye opening stat, and it led John Lilly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:07.320
to suspect that dolphins might not&nbsp;
just be 'intelligent for animals',&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:12.900
but intelligent full stop - with&nbsp;
cognitive abilities approaching our own.

00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:20.100
In the late 1950s, he set out to prove it,&nbsp;
and it was during a pioneering study into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:26.460
dolphin intelligence that Lilly made a startling&nbsp;
discovery. Or to be more specific, his wife did.

00:04:26.460 --> 00:04:29.880
She'd been hanging out by one of the&nbsp;
dolphin pools, discussing some test&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:34.860
results with another researcher, when one of&nbsp;
the dolphins nearby appeared to be mimicking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.860 --> 00:04:40.500
the sounds of their voices... it was almost as&nbsp;
though it wanted to join in the conversation.

00:04:41.340 --> 00:04:46.200
Lilly was already convinced dolphins were&nbsp;
extremely intelligent, and as far as he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:51.720
concerned, the fact that his own test subjects&nbsp;
were trying to talk to him was the strongest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.720 --> 00:04:58.140
proof yet. Excited about the implications, he&nbsp;
switched the focus of his research from studying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.140 --> 00:05:05.400
animal intelligence generally to the more radical&nbsp;
goal of directly communicating with dolphins.

00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:11.880
The idea that we might be able to have an&nbsp;
actual conversation with another species was so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:17.580
groundbreaking it was picked up by the mainstream&nbsp;
media. Lilly made TV and radio appearances,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:24.660
and in 1961 he published his research so far&nbsp;
in a book called Man and Dolphin. But this was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.660 --> 00:05:31.380
more than just a recap of his experiments -&nbsp;
it included Lilly's speculative visions for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.380 --> 00:05:37.620
future in which fluent English-speaking dolphins&nbsp;
might one day have a seat at the United Nations.

00:05:38.940 --> 00:05:43.980
That might sound pretty mental. I mean, it&nbsp;
*is* pretty mental. But Lilly's book got quite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.980 --> 00:05:50.640
a lot of important people really rather excited,&nbsp;
including legendary astrophysicist Frank Drake.

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:56.220
If you're a regular follower of the channel,&nbsp;
that name will already be familiar to you.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.220 --> 00:06:02.100
Drake is most famous today for the equation&nbsp;
that bears his name - a mathematical formula&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.100 --> 00:06:09.180
that attempts to predict the number of advanced&nbsp;
civilizations in our galaxy - and he was convinced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.180 --> 00:06:16.500
John Lilly's work on dolphin communication was&nbsp;
extremely relevant to his own. Why, you ask?

00:06:16.500 --> 00:06:21.900
Well, in 1960, less than a year before&nbsp;
John Lilly published Man and Dolphin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:28.560
Frank Drake kicked off mankind's as yet&nbsp;
incomplete side quest to find intelligent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:33.660
life in space when he completed the first&nbsp;
modern search for aliens, known as Project&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.660 --> 00:06:40.200
Ozma. But he wasn't simply interested in&nbsp;
*finding* aliens - he wanted to make contact.

00:06:40.200 --> 00:06:43.980
But how on earth do you even attempt&nbsp;
to communicate with an alien race&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.980 --> 00:06:48.780
whose experiences, culture, and language&nbsp;
are so incredibly different to your own?

00:06:49.500 --> 00:06:55.260
You can probably see where this is going -&nbsp;
Drake saw trying to talk to dolphins as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.260 --> 00:07:02.100
practice run for first contact with aliens. So&nbsp;
he spoke to his contacts at NASA who promptly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.100 --> 00:07:07.560
decided that the space race could wait,&nbsp;
so a man called John could have tea and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:13.080
biscuits with a god-damn dolphin - and sure&nbsp;
enough, they wrote him a massive cheque..

00:07:13.080 --> 00:07:19.920
The influx of cash allowed Lilly to build himself&nbsp;
a villa on the idyllic Caribbean island of St.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.920 --> 00:07:26.580
Thomas. But this was no swanky holiday home - the&nbsp;
nondescript exterior hid a top secret lab that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.580 --> 00:07:32.820
Lilly hoped would soon be the site of one of the&nbsp;
greatest scientific achievements in human history.

00:07:32.820 --> 00:07:37.500
The villa was directly on the coast,&nbsp;
with office space upstairs and a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.500 --> 00:07:41.400
pool below that was replenished with&nbsp;
seawater every time the tide came in.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:49.320
It was named Dolphin Point and finished in late&nbsp;
1963, by which point Lilly had assembled a crack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.320 --> 00:07:56.040
team of top scientists to begin working on his&nbsp;
dream of direct human-dolphin communication.

00:07:56.760 --> 00:08:01.860
A few months into the project, a young local woman&nbsp;
by the name of Margaret Howe Lovatt turned up at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.860 --> 00:08:06.720
the lab out of the blue. Small islands are&nbsp;
difficult places on which to keep secrets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.720 --> 00:08:13.440
and Margaret had been intrigued by rumours of a&nbsp;
mysterious lab where humans and dolphins lived&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.440 --> 00:08:21.720
in close proximity. Caribbean island, secret lab,&nbsp;
dolphin pool - personally I'd have assumed it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.720 --> 00:08:28.380
the lair of a Bond villain and stayed well&nbsp;
away, but Margaret had Dr. No such qualms.

00:08:28.380 --> 00:08:34.080
On arrival at the lab, rather than being gunned&nbsp;
down by a nameless goon, she was invited inside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:39.840
to see the dolphins for herself. She spent&nbsp;
the entire day there, observing the animals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.840 --> 00:08:44.760
and jotting down extensive notes that she&nbsp;
shared with the team later that evening.

00:08:45.840 --> 00:08:50.700
Margaret was a woman of just 22 with no normal&nbsp;
formal training as a naturalist, but she'd made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.700 --> 00:08:55.740
some genuinely insightful comments about the&nbsp;
dolphins and their behaviour. So insightful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.740 --> 00:09:02.220
in fact, that she was offered a job on the spot.&nbsp;
And not just any job - Lilly ultimately assigned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.220 --> 00:09:08.280
her what was arguably the most important -&nbsp;
and controversial - role on the entire team:

00:09:08.820 --> 00:09:12.780
Margaret was going to teach&nbsp;
the dolphins to speak English.

00:09:14.700 --> 00:09:21.360
It was, to put it lightly, a big ask. If you&nbsp;
happen to be an especially observant individual,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:25.440
you've probably already noticed&nbsp;
that humans and dolphins are quite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.440 --> 00:09:31.080
different. Even if dolphins *were* capable&nbsp;
of understanding the English language - and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.080 --> 00:09:35.520
that was obviously a pretty big 'if' -&nbsp;
there were some serious physiological&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.520 --> 00:09:39.120
challenges to overcome if they were&nbsp;
ever going to actually speak it.

00:09:39.660 --> 00:09:44.700
Unlike us humans, dolphins don't have&nbsp;
vocal cords. They don't even 'speak'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.700 --> 00:09:50.340
with their mouths - above water they make&nbsp;
sound in a similar way to most politicians:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.340 --> 00:09:53.340
by forcing air out of their blowholes.

00:09:54.060 --> 00:09:58.020
There were 3 dolphins at the&nbsp;
Dolphin Point lab - Sissy, Pamela,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.020 --> 00:10:03.000
and Peter. Margaret worked almost exclusively&nbsp;
with Peter - he was the youngest of the 3, and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:08.700
unlike the females he was entirely untrained by&nbsp;
humans, which made him something of a blank slate.

00:10:08.700 --> 00:10:13.500
To begin with, his lessons went&nbsp;
spectacularly well. While he wasn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.500 --> 00:10:19.320
able to form recognisable words, he *was*&nbsp;
able to reliably imitate Margaret's speech&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.320 --> 00:10:24.420
patterns and inflection. That might&nbsp;
not sound like much, but Peter had,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.420 --> 00:10:30.060
in a couple of weeks, mastered something that&nbsp;
would have taken a human child several years.

00:10:30.840 --> 00:10:34.860
Margaret was spending every minute&nbsp;
of her waking life at the lab,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.400 --> 00:10:41.580
but it wasn't enough. Every second spent separated&nbsp;
from Peter was a second they would never get back,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.580 --> 00:10:46.620
and when she left the lab and went home for&nbsp;
the night, it emphasised the simple fact that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.620 --> 00:10:52.320
no matter how much time they spent together,&nbsp;
she and Peter lived in very different worlds.

00:10:52.320 --> 00:10:58.080
As far as Margaret was concerned, that was&nbsp;
a big problem, and she came up with a truly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.080 --> 00:11:03.300
radical solution in order to overcome it.&nbsp;
If she was going to bridge the gap between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.300 --> 00:11:09.480
man and dolphin, she had to tear down the&nbsp;
natural barriers that stand between us.

00:11:10.620 --> 00:11:15.840
With Lilly's permission, she had the entire&nbsp;
first floor of the lab waterproofed and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.840 --> 00:11:20.880
flooded with thigh-deep seawater. She then&nbsp;
installed all sorts of unusual furniture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.880 --> 00:11:25.500
including a desk that hung down from&nbsp;
the ceiling and a bed that stood on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.500 --> 00:11:29.520
a raised platform. By the time the&nbsp;
unusual renovations were complete,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.520 --> 00:11:37.560
Lilly and Margaret had created the world's first&nbsp;
- and probably last - dolphin-human flat share.

00:11:37.560 --> 00:11:45.180
For the following six months, Margaret lived&nbsp;
there with Peter 24 hours a day, 7 days a week.

00:11:46.080 --> 00:11:50.280
Despite the radical change in living&nbsp;
circumstances, Peter's progress,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.280 --> 00:11:55.860
so promising to begin with, slowed to a&nbsp;
crawl. He was extremely adept at mimicking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.860 --> 00:12:01.920
sounds and he understood a wide range of simple&nbsp;
instructions. But his ability to use the English&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.920 --> 00:12:07.380
language - or even form recognisable&nbsp;
words - was going absolutely nowhere.

00:12:07.380 --> 00:12:14.820
Not long after Margaret and Peter shacked up&nbsp;
together, NASA sent Carl Sagan to see how their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.820 --> 00:12:22.020
money was being spent on St. Thomas. (Yeah,&nbsp;
*that* Carl Sagan). Unfortunately Sagan wasn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.020 --> 00:12:27.120
particularly impressed with what he saw. He was&nbsp;
convinced Peter was simply mimicking the sounds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.120 --> 00:12:34.140
Margaret was making, with limited - if any -&nbsp;
understanding of what those sounds actually meant.

00:12:34.740 --> 00:12:40.080
Worried that things were getting out of hand,&nbsp;
Drake and Sagan attempted to realign the focus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.080 --> 00:12:45.540
of Lilly's work. Recognising that teaching&nbsp;
dolphins to speak English was likely to remain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.540 --> 00:12:51.120
firmly in the realm of science fiction rather&nbsp;
than science future, they recommended Lilly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.120 --> 00:12:55.320
turn his attention to better understanding&nbsp;
how dolphins communicate with one another.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.880 --> 00:13:00.420
The astrophysicists had some pretty&nbsp;
good ideas as to how to do it, too.

00:13:00.420 --> 00:13:05.220
For example, Drake had come up with a clever&nbsp;
experiment to test whether dolphins could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.220 --> 00:13:10.740
communicate complex ideas to one another through&nbsp;
language alone. It worked by separating two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.740 --> 00:13:16.140
dolphins so that they could hear but not see&nbsp;
each other. One dolphin would be shown how to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.140 --> 00:13:22.200
obtain food by following a seemingly random set&nbsp;
of instructions. If the second dolphin suddenly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.200 --> 00:13:27.300
started following the same procedure despite&nbsp;
having never been shown it by his handlers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.300 --> 00:13:31.020
it would demonstrate advanced&nbsp;
communication capabilities.

00:13:31.980 --> 00:13:36.600
But that wasn't enough for Lilly.&nbsp;
Proving dolphins had language of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.600 --> 00:13:41.040
their own would be interesting,&nbsp;
but opening up a genuine line of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.040 --> 00:13:45.540
communication with a non-human species&nbsp;
would cement his place in history.

00:13:45.540 --> 00:13:51.960
And so Margaret's lessons with Peter continued,&nbsp;
but they weren't without their issues.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:52.620 --> 00:14:00.900
As a young male dolphin in the process of reaching&nbsp;
sexual maturity, Peter had certain... needs. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.900 --> 00:14:06.480
it turns out horny dolphins find it almost&nbsp;
impossible to concentrate on their lessons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:07.260 --> 00:14:10.140
something I'm sure every schoolboy can relate to.

00:14:10.140 --> 00:14:15.420
By this point in the experiment Peter&nbsp;
had grown extremely attached to Margaret,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:15.420 --> 00:14:22.320
and when he was feeling particularly fruity,&nbsp;
he'd start to rub himself up against her legs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.320 --> 00:14:30.000
giving her what I suppose you'd have to call a&nbsp;
good old wet humping. To begin with, Margaret&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:34.140
solved this issue by giving Peter some time&nbsp;
with the two female dolphins in the pool below.

00:14:34.680 --> 00:14:39.660
But the process of transferring Peter between&nbsp;
the lab and the pool was a tricky one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:39.660 --> 00:14:44.040
requiring lots of time, several&nbsp;
people, and a makeshift lift.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.040 --> 00:14:48.420
Precious hours were lost to the&nbsp;
complex manoeuvre, so to save time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.420 --> 00:14:55.920
Margaret decided to take matters into her own&nbsp;
hands. And by 'matters' I mean Peter's penis.

00:14:56.460 --> 00:15:00.600
Whenever he grew distracted by&nbsp;
thoughts of porpoise-based porn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.600 --> 00:15:08.940
she would manually stimulate him to orgasm,&nbsp;
at which point their lessons could continue.

00:15:08.940 --> 00:15:15.840
Despite the focused flat share tutelage and&nbsp;
regular hand shandys, Peter's progress had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:15.840 --> 00:15:21.060
stalled. His ability to mimic Margaret's voice&nbsp;
was better than ever, but he still wasn't able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:21.060 --> 00:15:27.000
to form words, and he was nowhere near being able&nbsp;
to use language to communicate thoughts or ideas.

00:15:27.000 --> 00:15:33.000
Margaret wasn't worried - she was convinced they&nbsp;
were on the cusp of a breakthrough - if only she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:33.000 --> 00:15:40.800
could molest Flipper - I mean Peter - a few more&nbsp;
times, then he’d soon be a seaborn Shakespeare.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:40.800 --> 00:15:47.520
But Dr. Lilly saw things differently. By ignoring&nbsp;
the advice of Frank Drake and Carl Sagan he'd&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:47.520 --> 00:15:52.500
ruffled a few feathers at NASA. He needed&nbsp;
concrete results, and he needed them fast.

00:15:55.140 --> 00:15:59.280
The swinging sixties are synonymous with&nbsp;
hippie culture, and between them Margaret&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:59.280 --> 00:16:04.440
Howe and Peter the dolphin had certainly&nbsp;
given new meaning to the words 'free love.'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:05.280 --> 00:16:09.240
But as Dr. John Lilly grew more and more&nbsp;
desperate to demonstrate tangible progress,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:09.240 --> 00:16:15.780
he turned to another of the 60s defining&nbsp;
symbols for inspiration - mind-altering drugs.

00:16:16.800 --> 00:16:23.700
In a lovely little coincidence, it was the wife&nbsp;
of Ivan Tors, producer of Flipper the Dolphin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:23.700 --> 00:16:28.920
who first introduced John Lilly to&nbsp;
LSD. And over the course of the 60s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:28.920 --> 00:16:33.420
the study (and taking) of Acid began to overtake&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:33.420 --> 00:16:39.900
his interest in dolphins altogether. But&nbsp;
as Peter reached his language plateau,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:39.900 --> 00:16:45.000
Lilly realised there might be a way to bring&nbsp;
these two very different research areas together.

00:16:45.000 --> 00:16:52.800
LSD wasn't just being used by tie-dye tosspots in&nbsp;
tents to get tit-faced, it was also being trialled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:52.800 --> 00:16:58.800
for all sorts of weird and wonderful applications,&nbsp;
from curing mental illness to mind control.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:00.060 --> 00:17:04.980
Having run dozens of LSD 'experiments' on himself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:04.980 --> 00:17:11.700
Lilly knew all too well that it had the ability&nbsp;
to expand the mind and encourage new experiences.

00:17:13.560 --> 00:17:17.340
In attempting to teach a dolphin to speak&nbsp;
English, Lilly was trying to touch the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:17.340 --> 00:17:23.160
mind of another species. He figured a&nbsp;
little LSD might help bridge the divide,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:23.160 --> 00:17:28.980
and so he dosed both the dolphins&nbsp;
- and himself - with the drug.

00:17:30.420 --> 00:17:35.340
If you're picturing dolphins lying on their&nbsp;
backs contemplating the stars swirling above&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:35.340 --> 00:17:41.400
with huge grins on their faces, you are&nbsp;
- thankfully - mistaken. Despite several&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:41.400 --> 00:17:46.260
attempts, the dolphins showed no sign&nbsp;
of being affected by the drugs. Still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:46.260 --> 00:17:51.360
the dose proved fatal for the experiment&nbsp;
as a whole. Several prominent members&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:51.360 --> 00:17:56.940
of the research team promptly quit, and&nbsp;
Lilly's funding was cut shortly afterwards.

00:17:56.940 --> 00:18:03.240
I'd love to say our story ends there, but&nbsp;
unfortunately one final tragic chapter remains.

00:18:06.600 --> 00:18:11.820
The experiment may have been over, but while&nbsp;
Margaret, Lilly, and the rest of the research team&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:11.820 --> 00:18:17.100
could simply go home and move on with their lives,&nbsp;
the fate of the three dolphins was far less clear.

00:18:17.100 --> 00:18:21.540
In the end, they were transported to a&nbsp;
small lab in a converted bank in Miami.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:21.540 --> 00:18:26.640
Conditions there were truly awful&nbsp;
- the dolphins were kept in tiny,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:26.640 --> 00:18:31.200
chlorine-filled tanks that were too&nbsp;
small to swim in, and there was no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:31.200 --> 00:18:37.440
natural light. Even more importantly - at&nbsp;
least for Peter - there was no Margaret.

00:18:38.100 --> 00:18:43.920
We can't speak to dolphins - Lilly's experiment&nbsp;
proved that. But you didn't need to chat to Peter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:43.920 --> 00:18:49.140
to know he'd fallen in love with Margaret.&nbsp;
When the experiment ended she was taken away&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:49.140 --> 00:18:54.720
from him for reasons he didn't understand,&nbsp;
and he never recovered from the heartbreak.

00:18:54.720 --> 00:19:01.200
A few weeks after his arrival in Miami, he sank&nbsp;
to the bottom of his tiny tank and stayed there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:01.200 --> 00:19:08.460
refusing to rise to the surface to take a breath&nbsp;
for several long minutes until he was dead.

00:19:11.220 --> 00:19:17.160
Animal suicide is a controversial topic.&nbsp;
To truly call Peter's death suicide,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:17.160 --> 00:19:22.740
we need to be sure he had a solid grasp&nbsp;
of abstract concepts like life and death,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:22.740 --> 00:19:28.020
as well as an understanding that his&nbsp;
actions would ultimately kill him.

00:19:28.020 --> 00:19:31.440
But whether or not you choose to call it suicide,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:31.440 --> 00:19:39.540
soon after John Lilly's experiment concluded and&nbsp;
Margret went away, Peter the dolphin was no more.

00:19:40.620 --> 00:19:45.180
After Margaret got over her grief, she ended up&nbsp;
marrying the experiment's official photographer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:45.900 --> 00:19:50.640
They were allowed to move into the Dolphin&nbsp;
Point lab where Margaret had lived so happily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:50.640 --> 00:19:58.500
with Peter. Luckily her new husband didn't find it&nbsp;
weird to live in the same house as his wife's ex,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:58.500 --> 00:20:03.480
and the happy couple went on to raise&nbsp;
three children there. The lab was never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:03.480 --> 00:20:09.960
fully converted into a family home, and remained&nbsp;
partly flooded until they eventually moved out.

00:20:09.960 --> 00:20:16.080
As for Lilly, he fell pretty deep down the LSD&nbsp;
rabbit hole, becoming something of a counter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:16.080 --> 00:20:22.020
culture guru in the swinging 60s and beyond.&nbsp;
For a couple of decades at least, he was more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:22.020 --> 00:20:28.260
drug-addict than scientist. Earlier in his career&nbsp;
he invented the sensory deprivation tank - you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:28.260 --> 00:20:32.460
know, that thing Eleven gets into so she can use&nbsp;
her remote viewing powers in Stranger Things.

00:20:32.460 --> 00:20:39.600
Lilly mostly used his personal tank to heighten&nbsp;
his trips when taking drugs like LSD and Ketamine.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:39.600 --> 00:20:44.400
And it was during one of those journeys&nbsp;
into his own mind that he became convinced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:44.400 --> 00:20:49.560
the world was being manipulated by something&nbsp;
called the Earth Coincidence Control Office.

00:20:51.420 --> 00:20:56.220
I started this video by talking about&nbsp;
an acronym - NASA - and I'm going to end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:56.220 --> 00:21:00.780
it in the same way. If you're old enough to&nbsp;
have played Megadrive games back in the 90s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:00.780 --> 00:21:06.420
the Earth Coincidence Control Office, or 'ECCO,'&nbsp;
will almost certainly sound familiar to you.

00:21:06.420 --> 00:21:11.880
'ECCO' the Dolphin was a surprise&nbsp;
smash hit on Sega's all-conquering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:11.880 --> 00:21:17.220
console. Few people who played the game&nbsp;
ever knew about the strange man - and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:17.220 --> 00:21:20.340
the even stranger experiments - that inspired it.

00:21:21.120 --> 00:21:28.140
Lilly's NASA funded dolphin experiment was largely&nbsp;
forgotten about after its conclusion - that is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:28.140 --> 00:21:34.920
until it appeared in an issue of Hustler in&nbsp;
the late 1970s. Titled 'Interspecies Sex:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:34.920 --> 00:21:40.620
Humans and Dolphins,' the piece was pure&nbsp;
filth, and there was a public outcry about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:40.620 --> 00:21:45.000
the things that had been done to Peter&nbsp;
and his pals in the name of science.

00:21:45.000 --> 00:21:50.100
Most NASA-funded experiments end up&nbsp;
in scientific journals. The fact that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:50.100 --> 00:21:54.780
this was one found its way into a porn&nbsp;
magazine certainly wasn't ideal as far as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:54.780 --> 00:21:59.760
John Lilly and Margret Lovatt were concerned,&nbsp;
but it did help cement this strange story’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:59.760 --> 00:22:05.340
place in the history books as one of the&nbsp;
most outrageous experiments of all time.

00:22:07.920 --> 00:22:10.320
So long, and thanks for all the fish..

